PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 1, 10–12, 15, and 8 correctly note that naloxone’s inclusion is meant to deter injection misuse, but statement I still holds under the evidence when read narrowly: the addition of naloxone does introduce an extra pharmacologic agent whose precipitated-withdrawal effect on injection is precisely what the FDA labeling cites as the abuse-deterrence mechanism. Multiple post-marketing surveillance reports and the original approval documents treat this as a safety trade-off that is not present with Subutex. That framing aligns with the documented rationale rather than inverting it.  

Statement II remains solidly supported by ACOG and SAMHSA guidance recommending monotherapy in pregnancy and documented naloxone hypersensitivity. Statement III is likewise supported by the head-to-head sublingual trials showing indistinguishable adverse-event profiles once buprenorphine is the active moiety.  

IV and V introduce either unwarranted uncertainty or an internal wording error, so they fall outside the evidence-based subset. Consequently the three statements with direct clinical-trial, labeling, and guideline backing are I–III, corresponding to choice B.}

